🧠 Git Flow: Professional Branching Model (Even for Solo Devs)
🌳 Branch Structure
main         ← always deployable (stable builds only)
develop      ← active integration of features
feature/*    ← new functionality (you work here daily)
hotfix/*     ← urgent fixes to main (later, if needed)
release/*    ← prep branch for production/staging
*/

✅ Your Ideal Workflow as a Solo Backend Dev
Step 1: Create the Core Branches (if not already)
git checkout -b develop
git push -u origin develop

git checkout -b feature/initial-backend-setup

Step 2: Work on Your Feature Branch

Write code, add projects, commit frequently:

git add .
git commit -m "Add initial project scaffolding and clean architecture setup"

Step 3: Merge Feature → Develop (Simulating Pull Request)
git checkout develop
git merge feature/initial-backend-setup
git push origin develop


💡 This mimics a pull request merge, even if you’re not opening PRs.

🔁 Repeat for All Backend Features

feature/auth-api

feature/expense-endpoints

feature/investment-module

Each one:

git checkout -b feature/xyz
# work, commit
git checkout develop
git merge feature/xyz
git push origin develop

🚀 When You're Ready to Deploy
git checkout main
git merge develop
git push origin main


Main should always reflect stable, production-ready code.

🧪 Pro Tips
Action	Command
List all branches	git branch -a
Delete merged feature	git branch -d feature/auth-api
Rename branch	git branch -m old-name new-name
Show branch tree	git log --oneline --graph --all
Create & switch branch	git checkout -b feature/xyz

